      "id": "hackernews-2026-05-16-a-good-lemma-is-worth-a-thousand-theorems-2007",
      "title": "A Good Lemma Is Worth a Thousand Theorems (2007)",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://sites.math.rutgers.edu/~zeilberg/Opinion82.html\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48157229\nPoints: 44\n# Comments: 9",
      "body": "A Good Lemma is Worth a Thousand Theorems\nTheorems are nice, but they are usually deadends. A lemma may be \"trivial\", or easy,  to prove,\nonce stated, but if it is good, its value far surpasses even the deepest theorems.\nWiki has a fairly long\nincluding the powerful Schur's Lemma, Lovasz's Local Lemma, and many others.\nBut my absolute favorite, that lead to at least two Fields medals (so far),\nIts significance was lauded in two consecutive MAA Hedrick lectures, last year\nby Tim Gowers, and this year\nby Jennifer Chayes. For example, the recent Green-Tao breakthrough, about\nprimes in arithmetic progressions, used a hypergraph extension of Szemeredi's\nis most famous for his\ntheorem, but his lemma is\nSo blessed be the lemmas, for they shall inherit mathematics. Even more important\nthan lemmas are observations, but that is another story.\nVince Vatter kindly pointed my attention to this funny\neconomist's critique of lemmas, but of course for them \"lemmas\" means \"complicated\".\nTodd Trimble kindly pointed out the following\nquote from Paul Taylor (\"Practical Foundations of Mathematics\" ,p. 192):\n\"Lemmas do the work in mathematics: Theorems, like\nmanagement, just take the credit. A good lemma also\nsurvives a philosophical or technological revolution.\"\nAdded Sept. 11, 2007: Kevin Buchin kindly tole me about the\nbeautiful woderful quote from THE BOOK.\nChapter \"Lattice Paths and Determinants\" in Aigner, Ziegler:\"Proofs from THE BOOK\"\nThe essence of mathematics is proving theorems – and so, that is what mathematicians\ndo: they prove theorems. But to tell the truth, what they really want\nto prove once in their lifetime, is a Lemma, like the one by Fatou in analysis,\nthe Lemma of Gauss in number theory, or the Burnside-Frobenius Lemma in\nNow what makes a mathematical statement a true Lemma? First, it should\nbe applicable to a wide variety of instances, even seemingly unrelated problems.\nSecondly, the statement should, once you have seen it, be completely obvious.\nThe reaction of the reader might well be one of faint envy: Why haven’t I noticed\nthis before? And thirdly, on an esthetic level, the Lemma including its proof\n(also in: Martin Aigner, Lattice Paths and Determinants,\nin H. Alt (Ed.): Computational Discrete Mathematics, LNCS 2122, pp. 1–12, 2001.)",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "susam",
      "publishedAt": "2026-05-16T05:56:41.000Z",
      "imageUrl": null,
      "originalUrl": "https://sites.math.rutgers.edu/~zeilberg/Opinion82.html",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
