      "id": "hackernews-2026-05-14-claude-for-legal",
      "title": "Claude for Legal",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://github.com/anthropics/claude-for-legal\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48141234\nPoints: 113\n# Comments: 106",
      "body": "Reference agents, skills, and data connectors for the legal workflows we see most — in-house commercial, privacy, product, corporate, employment, litigation, regulatory, AI governance, IP, and the learning side of the practice (law school clinics and students).\nNew here? Start with QUICKSTART.md — install in 60 seconds. This README is the full reference.\nEverything here is available two ways from one source: install it as a Claude Cowork or Claude Code plugin, or deploy it through the Claude Managed Agents API behind your own workflow engine. Same system prompt, same skills — you choose where it runs.\nGet access to Claude Cowork\nFollow the instructions in the video below:\nImportantEvery output from these plugins is a draft for attorney review — not legal advice, not a legal conclusion, not a substitute for a lawyer. They are built with guardrails that reflect that: source attribution on every citation, conservative defaults on privilege and subjective legal calls, jurisdiction assumptions surfaced, and explicit gates before anything is filed, sent, or relied on. A lawyer reviews, verifies, and takes professional responsibility for anything that leaves the building. These plugins make that review faster; they do not replace it.\nThese plugins do not represent Anthropic's legal positions. They are tools that help lawyers analyze issues. Where a skill includes a checklist item, a suggested framework, a risk flag, or a characterization of case law or regulatory guidance, that is an aid to the reviewing attorney's own analysis, not a statement of Anthropic's view of the law. The law in many of these areas is unsettled and evolving. The attorney using the plugin — not the plugin, and not Anthropic — is responsible for the legal positions taken in their work product.\nPractice-area plugins covering in-house, firm, and academic legal work — each one built around a cold-start interview that learns your playbook and a CLAUDE.md practice profile that every skill reads from.\nManaged-agent cookbooks for the scheduled, eyes-on-the-feed workflows (renewal watcher, docket watcher, regulatory feed monitor, diligence grid, launch radar).\nMCP connectors across general productivity (Slack, Google Drive, Box) and legal-specific systems (Ironclad, DocuSign, iManage, Everlaw, CourtListener, and more).\nNamed agents — end-to-end workflow agents (Vendor Agreement Reviewer, DSAR Responder, Termination Reviewer, Claim Chart Builder, …) with job-style names and a single command to run each one.\nEach agent is named for the workflow it runs. They're the most common surface — start with the ones that match your work, then tune the underlying skill, the practice profile, and the connectors to how your team does it.\nReviews a vendor MSA against your playbook and produces a redline memo\nGREEN/YELLOW/RED triage of inbound NDAs so only the hard ones hit a lawyer's desk\nTraces how a contract has changed across its base agreement and every amendment\nScans the contract register for cancel-by and renewal deadlines\nWeekly sweep of signed agreements with playbook deviations — prompts the attorney to log context while memory is fresh\nWatches the deviation log and proposes playbook updates when a clause has drifted\nRoutes contract issues to the right approver and drafts the ask\nTabular review over a data room with one row per document and every cell cited\nReads VDR documents and extracts issues per house categories and materiality thresholds\nDrafts unanimous written consents in house format with precedent search\nBuilds the disclosure schedule from diligence findings against the purchase-agreement threshold\nComputes filing deadlines across jurisdictions and entity types, runs health audits\nTracks every condition, consent, document, and filing blocking close\nPhased post-closing integration plan with consent tracking and weekly status\nMonitors the VDR for new uploads and posts closing checklist status on schedule\nRuns a proposed termination against jurisdiction-specific risk flags\nReviews offer letters and restrictive covenants with a jurisdiction check\nTests a proposed engagement against the controlling state test\nMonitors open leaves with FMLA/CFRA/PFL/ADA deadlines and decision-point alerts\nOpens, tracks, adds to, and summarizes internal investigation matters\nDrafts employment policies with state supplements where law differs\nKicks off EOR-vs-entity planning and outside-counsel briefing for a new country\nJurisdiction-aware employment Q&A for the \"quick question\" channel\nDrafts DSAR acknowledgments and substantive responses within statutory timelines\nReviews a DPA against your playbook as controller or processor\nGenerates a Privacy Impact Assessment in house format for a new feature or activity\nDecides whether a processing activity needs a PIA, a mandatory GDPR DPIA, or can proceed\nSweeps saved PIAs, DPA reviews, and triage results for policy drift\nReviews a product launch against your risk calibration\nFlags copy that needs substantiation, reframing, or cutting\n\"Is this a problem?\" Triage\nFast answer for the quick Slack question — pattern-matches your calibration\nWatches the launch tracker for upcoming launches that need legal review\nPolls regulatory feeds and writes the Monday-morning digest\nCheck regulatory feeds now and report what's new since last check\nDiffs a specific regulatory change against the indexed policy library\nOpen gaps tracker — what's flagged and not yet closed\nMarked-up policy redraft closing a gap — a proposal for the policy owner's review, not a direct edit to source documents\nReview open NPRM comment periods, log decisions, track deadlines\nClassifies proposed AI use cases against your registry\nRuns an AIA across the regimes in scope\nReviews vendor AI terms for training-on-data, liability, model-change, and policy gaps\nDiffs a new AI regulation against your current governance posture\nSweeps saved AIAs, triage results, and vendor reviews for AI-policy drift\nFirst-pass clearance with knockout check and confusion heuristics\nDrafts or triages a C&D, calibrated to your enforcement posture\nDrafts a takedown, triages one received, or drafts a §512(g) counter-notice\nClassifies open source licenses against your deployment model\nStructured first look at potentially blocking patents — triage, not an opinion\nTriage across TM / copyright / patent / trade secret — factors, not a finding\nReviews assignment, ownership, license grants, warranties, and indemnities\nRegistrations, renewals, maintenance fees, use declarations\nScheduled deadline report from the IP portfolio register\nElement-by-element claim chart, patent or civil cause of action\nMonitors court dockets for filings and deadlines\nDrafts a demand with FRE 408 awareness and a send gate\nPre-drafting context gathering — parties, facts, basis, leverage, privilege\nTriages an inbound demand — options, portfolio cross-check, handoff\nClassifies, scopes, and plans compliance with a new subpoena\nBuilds or updates a chronology from declared sources and uploads\nBuilds a deposition outline tied to case theory with docs and impeachment\nDrafts a brief section in house style, consistent with case theory\nFirst-pass privilege log review — obvious calls + flags for attorney review\nIssue, refresh, release, or report on legal holds\nUniform intake for a new matter — writes matter.md, history.md, appends to log\nDeep briefing on one matter — ready for a GC or outside counsel call\nRisk distribution, upcoming deadlines, stale matters\nGenerates weekly status-request drafts across the active portfolio\nStructured client intake with cross-area issue spotting and conflict flags\nIRAC-scaffolded case analysis memo with research gaps flagged\nStatutes to check, case law areas, Westlaw search terms — leads, not cites\nAdd, report, update, and close case deadlines with malpractice-aware warnings\nCase status by audience — client, professor, or court-ready\nRoutine client correspondence — appointment confirms, doc requests, updates\nSemester onboarding — clinic procedures, tool walkthrough, practice exercises\nEnd-of-semester case handoff memos — the mirror of ramp\nProfessor's review queue (when formal review supervision is configured)\nJurisdiction-aware MBE and essay practice targeted at weak subjects\nIt asks, you answer, it pushes back — does not give you the answer\nGrades your IRAC essay on structure, issue-spotting, rules, analysis\nBrief a case in your preferred format\nBuild or extend an outline in your format from class notes and casebook\nPredicts professor's questions and drills them before class\nAnalyze past exams from the same professor; forecast likely emphases\nStructural feedback on a draft — never rewrites\nGenerate or drill flashcards — Leitner-style buckets\nLong-term study plan with scheduled sessions, adaptive to session history\nSearch watched registries for community legal skills\nInstall a community skill with trust checks and skills-QA\nEvaluate a skill against the Legal Skill Design Framework\nSuggest community skills based on recent activity in other plugins\nCheck for updates to installed community skills\nPeriodic check of watched registries for new and updated skills\nFor Managed Agent deployment — agent.yaml, leaf-worker subagents, steering-event examples, and per-agent security notes — see managed-agent-cookbooks/.\ncommercial-legal/         # in-house commercial — vendor/NDA/SaaS review, renewals, escalations\ncorporate-legal/          # M&A diligence, closing checklists, board consents, entity compliance\nemployment-legal/         # hire/term review, worker classification, leave, investigations\nprivacy-legal/            # DPA, DSAR, PIA, privacy triage, policy monitor\nproduct-legal/            # launch review, marketing claims, \"is this a problem?\" triage\nregulatory-legal/         # reg feed watcher, policy diff, gap tracker, NPRM comments\nai-governance-legal/      # AI use-case triage, AIAs, vendor AI review, AI reg gap-check\nip-legal/                 # trademark clearance, FTO, C&D, DMCA, OSS, IP clauses, portfolio\nlitigation-legal/         # portfolio, matters, holds, demands, depo prep, claim charts\nlegal-clinic/             # clinic setup, student ramp, intake, deadlines, memos, handoffs\nlaw-student/              # Socratic drilling, outlining, IRAC, bar prep, flashcards\nlegal-builder-hub/        # community skill discovery and install with a trust gate\nexternal_plugins/         # partner-built plugins maintained by their vendors\ncocounsel-legal/        # Thomson Reuters — Westlaw Deep Research via the CoCounsel Legal MCP\nmanaged-agent-cookbooks/  # Claude Managed Agent cookbooks — one dir per scheduled agent\nscripts/                  # deploy-managed-agent.sh · validate.py · orchestrate.py · lint-tool-scope.py\nEach plugin directory has the same shape:\nCLAUDE.md               # template practice profile — filled in by /<plugin>:cold-start-interview\nskills/                 # skills — each is a /<plugin>:<skill> slash command\nagents/                 # scheduled agents (if any)\nhooks/                  # pre- and post-tool hooks (if any)\nClick Customize in the left sidebar.\nClick Browse plugins and install the ones you want, or upload a custom plugin file (any plugin directory zipped up).\nAfter install, skills fire automatically when relevant, slash commands are available via /, and the scheduled agents run on the cadence set in their frontmatter.\n# Add the marketplace (use the absolute path to this repo or a GitHub URL)\n# Install a plugin — pick the ones that match your practice\n# Restart Claude Code, then run setup for each plugin you installed.\n# This writes your practice profile to ~/.claude/plugins/config/claude-for-legal/<plugin>/CLAUDE.md\nRun the cold-start interview first. Every other skill in a plugin reads from the practice profile it writes. Skipping setup is the single most common reason a skill produces generic output. The interview takes 10–20 minutes per plugin and will ask you to point at seed documents (a signed MSA, a playbook, a prior review memo — whatever fits the plugin). More seed material is better; a quick start option is available if you want to be productive in 2 minutes and refine later.\nStart by connecting a research tool. Everything else is better with one, and citations are unverified without one. See MCP Connectors below for the full list — CourtListener, Trellis, Descrybe, and Solve Intelligence are the research tools the citation guardrails look for.\nFor the scheduled agents — regulatory feed monitor, renewal watcher, docket watcher, diligence grid, launch radar — deploy behind your own orchestrator:\nEach template under managed-agent-cookbooks/ references the same system prompt and skills as its plugin counterpart. The deploy script resolves file references, uploads skills, creates leaf-worker subagents, and POSTs the orchestrator to /v1/agents. See scripts/orchestrate.py for a reference event loop that routes handoff_request events between agents via your own orchestration layer.\nResearch Preview: subagent delegation (callable_agents) is a preview capability and supports a single delegation level. See per-agent READMEs for security tier and handoff guidance.\nSelf-contained practice-area bundles — skills, agents, hooks, and a template practice profile. Install the ones you need.\nDomain expertise, conventions, and step-by-step methods Claude draws on automatically when relevant — and slash actions you trigger explicitly: /commercial-legal:review, /privacy-legal:dsar-response, /litigation-legal:claim-chart.\nScheduled or event-driven workflows (renewal watcher, docket watcher, reg-change monitor). Runs in the background, posts to a channel or writes a file.\nPlain-English CLAUDE.md describing your playbook, escalation rules, and house style. Every skill reads from it.\nMCP servers that wire Claude to your data — CLM, DMS, e-discovery, research platforms, productivity.\nagent.yaml + depth-1 subagents + steering examples for headless deployment.\nEverything is markdown and JSON. No build step.\nGrouped by where the work sits. Each plugin's cold-start interview is what tailors it to your team — start there.\nPlaybook-aware review of vendor agreements, NDAs, and SaaS subscriptions. Amendment tracing. Renewal register with cancel-by alerts. Escalation routing. Stakeholder summaries.\nM&A diligence with tabular review and citation-per-cell. Disclosure schedules, closing checklists, written consents, board minutes. Entity compliance tracker. Post-close integration.\nPrivacy triage (PIA vs DPIA vs proceed), PIA generation, DPA review as controller or processor, DSAR response. Policy monitor watches drift between policy and practice.\nLaunch review against house risk calibration. Marketing claims check. \"Is this a problem?\" triage for Slack questions. Feature risk assessment.\nHire and termination review with jurisdiction-specific flags. Worker classification. Leave tracker (FMLA/CFRA/PFL/ADA). Internal investigations. Policy drafting with state supplements.\nAI use-case triage against your registry. Impact assessments across regimes in scope. Vendor AI review. Reg-to-policy gap analysis.\nRegulatory feed watcher, policy diff, gaps tracker, NPRM comment-period tracker. The Monday-morning digest your team actually reads.\nTrademark clearance, FTO triage, C&D drafting and triage, DMCA takedown and counter-notice, OSS compliance, IP clause review, portfolio tracking.\nWorks two surfaces. In-house/portfolio: matter intake, portfolio status, legal holds, outside counsel status, demands. Firm/solo: chronology building, claim charts (patent and civil), deposition prep, privilege log review, brief drafting.\nSocratic drilling, case briefing, outline building, IRAC grading, cold-call prep, flashcards, bar prep, exam forecasting, study planning. Learning mode, not answer mode — it never writes the answer for you.\nProfessor setup and student semester ramp. Per-practice-area supervisor guide with pedagogy posture (assist / guide / teach). Structured intake with cross-area issue spotting. Deadline tracking with malpractice-aware caution. Memo scaffolds, client letters (routine + plain-language), semester handoffs. Built within ABA Formal Op. 512.\nCommunity skill discovery and install with a real trust layer — watched registries, a QA framework (/legal-builder-hub:skills-qa), SHA-pinned updates, and a mandatory trust check before anything lands in your environment.\nPlugins under external_plugins/ are built and maintained by their vendors. They install from this marketplace like any other plugin, but the vendor owns the code, the connector, and the support channel.\nWestlaw Deep Research with fully cited reports — caselaw, statutes, regulations, Practical Law, and secondary sources across up to three U.S. jurisdictions per run. Requires a CoCounsel Legal subscription with the MCP connector enabled. Support: cocounselsupport@tr.com.\nThe trust layer for community legal skills\nThe community is building legal skills fast — registries like LegalOps Consulting's lpm-skills and Lawvable already list dozens. But nobody certifies community skills, and a lawyer installing a random skill from GitHub is installing code that runs with access to their matter files, their practice profile, and their research connectors.\nlegal-builder-hub gives the ecosystem the trust layer it's missing:\nSecurity review — hidden-content scan, injection detection, structural trust check on every install\nAllowlist — restrictive-by-default source gate (registries, publishers, connectors, licenses)\nLicense gate — deployment-context-aware license policy (personal / firm-internal / product-embedding)\nFreshness gate — tracks whether bundled reference content (regulations, statutes, procedures) has passed its verification window, and warns at invocation\nRe-scan at update — a skill that was clean at v1.0 and poisoned at v1.1 gets caught\nInstall log — an auditable record of what's installed, from where, under what license, with what review verdict\nThe allowlist is restrictive by default. Permissive mode is an explicit choice. A non-lawyer gets routed to their attorney contact, not an \"install anyway\" button.\nCommunity skills go through the same design review (/legal-builder-hub:skills-qa) as the first-party plugins. If you build for lawyers, run the QA against your own skill before publishing. It's the review a lawyer would do if they could read code.\nImportantConnect a research tool first. Every plugin ships with legal research connectors already configured — CourtListener, Trellis, Descrybe, Solve Intelligence, and others depending on practice area. You authorize them once, and from then on Claude pulls from authoritative sources and verifies its citations against current databases instead of relying on training knowledge. Citations that come through a research connector are tagged with the source. Citations from model knowledge alone are flagged [verify] and, if no research tool is connected at all, the reviewer note above the deliverable records that sources weren't verified so you know to check. The connectors are what make the cites trustworthy — set them up before you set up anything else.\nThese plugins ship connectors for the systems legal teams live in. A connector gives Claude the ability to read from and (where scoped) write to your data; the skills and commands use them.\nRead channels, search, send messages and canvases\nRead docs, sheets, slides; fetch by link\nWestlaw Deep Research — cited reports across caselaw, statutes, regulations, Practical Law\nRead files and folders in VDRs and matter rooms\nRead the contract register, renewal dates, clauses\nEnvelope status, executed contracts, CLM metadata\nRead from the DMS — matter workspaces, document versions\nE-discovery productions, tagged sets, chronologies\nState court dockets and motions\nClinic-style matter management and calendaring\nIn-document drafting and defined-terms checks\nContract review assist and clause libraries\nCase law research and summarization\nMatter routing and outside counsel panel\nConnectors marked \"customer subscription\" need the customer's own account and API key. Configure them in each plugin's .mcp.json or via claude mcp in your Claude Code setup.\nBuilding a connector? See CONNECTORS.md for what a good legal MCP server looks like and how to submit yours for inclusion.\nLawyers live in Word and Excel. Every contract-touching skill in this repo is authored to work in the Claude for Word sidebar, with tracked changes as the output mode. That's commercial-legal:review (vendor agreements, NDAs, SaaS subscriptions), commercial-legal:amendment-history, ip-legal:ip-clause-review, ai-governance-legal:vendor-ai-review, privacy-legal:dpa-review, and the diligence extraction in corporate-legal. A reviewer accepts or rejects each change exactly as they would for a human markup — numbering, defined terms, cross-references, and styles are preserved.\nThe Excel-facing skills produce workbooks that open cleanly: corporate-legal:tabular-review writes a multi-sheet .xlsx with a sources sheet, litigation-legal:claim-chart writes an element-by-element claim chart with citation columns, corporate-legal:entity-compliance writes the compliance register with deadline columns, and commercial-legal:renewal-tracker exports the renewal register sorted by cancel-by date.\nInstall Claude for Microsoft 365 from Microsoft AppSource. Once installed, the skills from any plugin you've enabled are available from the sidebar via /, and connectors are reachable from the same surface. A single thread can span Word, Excel, PowerPoint, and Outlook.\nFor IT admins deploying the add-in against your own cloud (Vertex AI, Bedrock, or an internal gateway) rather than Anthropic's API, see the separate claude-for-msft-365-install tooling.\nThese are reference templates. They get better when you tune them to how your team works — and the customization mechanism is the plugin itself, not a config file buried in a repo.\nRun the cold-start interview. It is the customization mechanism. It asks how your practice works, reads your seed documents, and writes your practice profile. Every other skill reads from that profile. A /commercial-legal:cold-start-interview with five signed MSAs, your playbook, and your escalation matrix will make the review skills noticeably sharper.\nEdit the practice profile. Your profile lives at ~/.claude/plugins/config/claude-for-legal/<plugin>/CLAUDE.md. Edit it directly for small fixes — a wrong escalation threshold, a new integration, a policy update. It survives plugin updates.\nRe-run setup. /<plugin>:cold-start-interview again for a full re-interview when your practice shifts materially (new jurisdiction, new CLM, new policy).\nSwap connectors. Point .mcp.json at your CLM, DMS, e-discovery platform, launch tracker, HRIS. Skills fall back gracefully when a connector isn't configured — no silent no-ops.\nBring your playbook and templates. Drop your terminology, house style, and branded templates into the plugin's CLAUDE.md and references/. The skills will pick them up.\nFork skills for house style. Every skill is a markdown file under skills/. Edit the steps, the gates, the output format.\nAdd scheduled agents. The agents under <plugin>/agents/ are markdown with a cron-style schedule. Add your own for the watchers your team needs.\nNo build step. Everything is markdown and JSON.\nThe full map across all plugins. The cold-start interview is the first thing to run in any plugin.\nCold-start — learns your AI governance practice\nClassify AI use case — approved, conditional, or no\nRun an AI impact assessment in house format\nReview vendor AI terms against governance positions\nDiff a new AI regulation against your governance posture\nKeep the AI policy current with practice\nDraft a firm AI usage policy from published model policies, adapted to your practice profile\nPractice profile interview and starter-pack recommendation\nSearch watched registries for community legal skills\nInstall a community skill with trust checks\nEvaluate a skill against the Design Framework\nSuggest community skills from activity in other plugins\nCheck for updates to installed community skills\nDisable a community skill without removing files\nUninstall a community skill installed via the hub\nPeriodic check of watched registries for updates\nProfessor setup — areas, jurisdiction, supervision style\nProfessor practice-area guide — intake, pedagogy posture, review gates\nStudent semester onboarding with practice exercises\nStructured intake with cross-area issue spotting\nLog a client communication — append-only per-case record\nResearch roadmap — statutes, case law, search terms\nIRAC-scaffolded analysis memo with research gaps flagged\nFirst draft of a common clinic document\nRoutine client correspondence from templates\nCase status by audience — client, professor, court-ready\nTrack case deadlines with malpractice-aware warnings\nProfessor's review queue (if formal supervision)\nCold-start — learn your commercial contracts practice\nvendor-agreement-review · nda-review · saas-msa-review\nReview vendor agreement, NDA, or SaaS subscription\nTrace contract changes across base and amendments\nShow contracts with cancel-by deadlines within 90 days\nRoute a contract issue and draft the ask\nReview and approve pending playbook update proposals\nTranslates a review into a business-stakeholder summary\nWeekly sweep of the renewal register\nWeekly surface of signed agreements with deviations\nProposes playbook updates when a clause has drifted\nHouse cold-start, with optional --new-deal kickoff\nTabular review — one row per document, every cell cited\nExtract issues from VDR documents per house thresholds\nBuild material contracts disclosure schedule\nWhat's blocking close with critical path\nDraft board or committee consent in house format\nEntity compliance tracker across jurisdictions\nPost-closing integration tracker with consent tracking\nDrafts board or committee minutes in house format\nAggregates diligence findings into a deal briefing\nDetects Luminance/Kira, QAs bulk-tool output\nMonitors VDR uploads and posts checklist status\nCold-start — learns jurisdictions and escalation rules\nJurisdiction-aware wage/hour and employment Q&A\nReview offer letter and restrictive covenants\nTermination review with high-risk flag detection\nClassify a proposed engagement against the state test\nDraft employment policy with state supplements\nCheck open leaves for deadline alerts\nAdd a new leave to the leave register\nOpen a new internal investigation matter\nAdd data to an open investigation — docs, notes\nDraft or update the privileged investigation memo\nAsk questions against an open investigation log\nDraft audience-specific summary from investigation memo\nKick off expansion planning for a new country\nUpdate status of an in-progress expansion project\nDiffs handbook changes and flags state supplement impacts\nWeekly monitor of open leaves with hard deadlines\nCold-start — learn your IP practice and posture\nTrademark clearance first pass — knockout + similar marks\nFreedom-to-operate triage, not an FTO opinion\nDraft a C&D or triage one you received\nDMCA notice, response triage, or §512(g) counter-notice\nInfringement triage across all four IP rights\nReview IP clauses — assignment, license, warranties\nOpen source license compliance check\nTrack IP portfolio deadlines and renewals\nWeekly report of IP portfolio deadlines\nCold-start — risk, landscape, house brief style\nIntake a new matter — writes matter.md and history\nDeep briefing on one matter for a call\nAppend a dated event to a matter's history\nPortfolio rollup — risk, deadlines, stale matters\nClose a matter — archive, retain record\nPre-drafting context — parties, facts, leverage\nDraft demand letter with FRE 408 gate and .docx output\nTriage inbound demand — options, portfolio cross-check\nTriage subpoena — scope, burden, privilege, plan\nIssue, refresh, release, or report on legal holds\nWeekly status-request emails to outside counsel\nElement chart — patent or civil cause of action\nBuild or update a chronology from sources and uploads\nDeposition outline tied to case theory\nFirst-pass privilege log review with flags\nDraft a brief section in house style\nMonitors court dockets for filings and deadlines\nCold-start — learns your privacy practice\nDetermine PIA vs GDPR DPIA vs proceed\nGenerate a Privacy Impact Assessment in house format\nReview a DPA — auto-detects controller vs processor\nWalk a DSAR and draft response — verify, locate, assess\nDiff a regulation against current policy and practice\nCold-start — connects launch tracker, learns calibration\nFast \"is this a problem?\" answer for quick questions\nFull launch review against framework and calibration\nReview marketing copy for claims that need work\nDeep-dive risk on a single feature when launch review flags\nMonitors launch tracker for upcoming reviews\nCold-start — watchlist, policy index, materiality\nCheck regulatory feeds now and report what's new\nDiff a regulatory change against the policy library\nOpen gaps tracker — what's flagged and not closed\nMarked-up policy redraft closing a gap — proposal for the policy owner's review\nReview open NPRM comment periods and deadlines\nScheduled regulatory feed sweep with materiality filter\nAbout-you interview — classes, bar, learning style\nSocratic drill — it asks, you answer, it pushes back\nBrief a case in your preferred format\nBuild or extend an outline in your format\nGrade IRAC essay — structure, issues, rules, analysis\nStructural feedback on your writing — never rewrites\nPredict professor's questions and drill them\nMBE or essay questions targeted at weak subjects\nGenerate or drill flashcards — Leitner-style\nAnalyze past exams to forecast likely emphases\nBuild or update a long-term study plan\nRun a focused N-question session; update the plan\nRun Westlaw Deep Research — start, poll, and present a fully cited report\nEverything here is markdown and JSON. Fork, edit, PR.\nNew skill → add it under <plugin>/skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md with the frontmatter the existing skills use (name, description, argument-hint). Keep the description under 1024 characters — it's the trigger signal. The skill is invokable as /<plugin>:<skill-name>. Mark pure-reference skills user-invocable: false.\nNew agent → add <plugin>/agents/<name>.md with scheduling frontmatter and the system prompt. Add a matching managed-agent-cookbooks/<name>/ if you want headless deployment.\nCommunity skills → use /legal-builder-hub:skill-installer to test a community skill in your environment. The hub runs /legal-builder-hub:skills-qa against every skill before installing — it scores the skill against the Legal Skill Design Framework (nine design parameters, three legal failure modes, a trust-surface check) and rejects anything that fails.\nValidate cookbooks before pushing → bash scripts/test-cookbooks.sh dry-runs every managed-agent cookbook and lints orchestrator tool scope.\nLicensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.",
