      "id": "bbc-2026-05-09-tech-now",
      "title": "Tech Now",
      "summary": "Andrew Rogers explores new tech trials helping people stay underwater for longer.",
      "body": "Home            News            Sport            Weather            iPlayer            Sounds            Bitesize     CBeebies     CBBC     Food         Home     News     Sport     Business     Technology     Health     Culture     Arts     Travel     Earth     Audio     Video     Live             Home            News            Sport            Weather            iPlayer            Sounds            Bitesize     CBeebies     CBBC     Food        Home     News     Sport     Business     Technology     Health     Culture     Arts     Travel     Earth     Audio     Video     Live         Close menu\nFIFA World CupA-ZTV GuideWatchlistHomeTech NowDeep Dive: Underwater TechTech Now - Deep Dive: Underwater TechAndrew Rogers explores the latest tech being trialled to help people stay underwater for longer and Joe Tidy reports on a huge U-turn for big tech as Instagram stops end-to-end encryption. Duration25 minsFirst shown9 May 2026Available for 11 monthsAudio DescribedSign LanguageEpisodes EpisodesAdrienne Murray explores how new 3D imaging is supporting heart procedures.This episodeDeep Dive: Underwater TechArt Mystery: Can AI Solve It? Harriet Bradshaw finds out how AI is being used to help solve an art history mystery. Blenheim Palace: Can Tech Help to Restore it?Shiona McCallum tours Blenheim Palace, exploring the tech aiding its restoration.Climate Science in the CourtroomHow research linking climate change and extreme weather events is being used in lawsuits.Inside the High-Tech Insect FarmAlasdair Keane visits the underground insect farm turning food waste into animal feed.MWC Barcelona: What's New In Phone Tech?Paul Carter checks out the newest phones, gadgets and trends from MWC Barcelona. Norway's Fjords: Can Tourism Go Green?Alasdair Keane climbs aboard an electric boat in Norway.Inside Europe’s Space RaceAdrienne Murray visits the Esrange Spaceport in the extreme north of Sweden.Winter Olympics: Can Tech Boost Performance?Alasdair Keane visits the Olympic training centre in Oslo.Future Farming in AustraliaNick Kwek meets innovators using tech for Australia's agricultural industry.From Las Vegas, the latest trends and innovations at CES 2026.Jacqui Wakefield joins singer KT Tunstall as she sees her musical avatar.Space Exploration: Can Robots Lead the Way?Nick Kwek visits the National Space Industry Hub in Sydney.Icebreaker Tech: Navigating the ArcticAdrienne Murray meets the engineers designing icebreaker ship technology for the Arctic.Rising Seas: Can Tech Help Us Prepare?The scientists on a quest to improve our predictions of global sea level rise.Fixing Fashion's Sizing ProblemShiona McCallum meets an AI startup working to fix erratic clothing sizes in fashion.Movie Tech: Behind the ScenesAlasdair Keane explores a high-tech studio that transforms how car stunts are made.Brain Cells and BiocomputingZoe Kleinman meets the scientists trying to make living computers using brain cells.Cloud Seeding and Conspiracy TheoriesMarco Silva unpacks the science and technology of cloud seeding.IFA Berlin: The Rise of Smart TechAlasdair Keane explores the tech highlights at IFA Berlin.Lara Lewington explores the tech being trialled to find 'hidden' pancreatic cancer cases.Fighting Wildfires: Can Tech Help?How technology is bringing hope of earlier detection and prevention to wildfires.AI and the Future of HumanityJoe Tidy explores how the rise of artificial intelligence may play out.Sagrada Família: Can Tech Help Complete It?Yasmin Morgan-Griffiths has exclusive access to La Sagrada Familia in Barcelona.Lithium Mining: The ImpactIone Wells explores if lithium mining can be done more sustainably.Exploring some of the latest tech innovations in the world of sport.Ione Wells explores the search for Chile's stolen children.Rubin Observatory: Mapping the UniverseIone Wells visits the Rubin Observatory in Chile's Atacama Desert.Super Science: Inside ESSAdrienne Murray heads behind the scenes at the European Spallation Source in Sweden.Embryo Screening: Designing Life?A new form of testing claims to predict how likely an unborn child is to develop diseases.",
